WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.220
What makes a computational task

2
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.260
hard? We will identify the

3
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.240
required output, derive the work

4
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.740
of printing a Hanoi solution,

5
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.840
and distinguish decision questions from

6
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.260
optimisation objectives.

7
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.300
A task can be difficult

8
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.460
to understand or code without

9
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.440
having a bad asymptotic running

10
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.580
time. Complexity analysis instead asks

11
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.380
how the required resources grow

12
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.180
as the encoded input grows,

13
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.960
with a clear statement of

14
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.520
what the algorithm must produce.

15
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.040
For Towers of Hanoi, move

16
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.060
disks between three pegs without

17
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.700
placing a larger disk on

18
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.620
a smaller one. The standard

19
00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.660
recursive method transfers the smaller

20
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.680
stack, moves the largest disk,

21
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.560
and transfers the smaller stack

22
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:41.840
again.

23
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.160
With three disks, label the

24
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.120
source A, destination B and

25
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.080
spare C. The seven moves

26
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.540
are A to B, A

27
00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:50.660
to C, B to C,

28
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:51.540
A to B, C to

29
00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:52.480
A, C to B and

30
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:53.820
A to B, with each

31
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.420
move involving the only legal

32
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:56.640
top disk for that step.

33
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.540
The move count has base

34
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.020
one for one disk and

35
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.560
recurrence M of n equals

36
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.840
twice M of n minus

37
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.100
one plus one. Expanding gives

38
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.580
two to the n minus

39
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.560
one, so printing every move

40
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.140
necessarily requires time proportional to

41
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.040
that exponentially long output.

42
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.640
Computing only the number of

43
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.100
moves is a different output

44
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.720
task from listing them all.

45
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.240
The binary representation of two

46
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.420
to the n minus one

47
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.500
has only n bits, so

48
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.160
an output-size argument about the

49
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.440
full move list cannot be

50
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.300
transferred unchanged to returning that

51
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:30.620
count.

52
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.260
The slide's numerical estimate for

53
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.880
sixty-four disks is incorrect, so

54
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.220
retain the exact move-count derivation

55
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.340
rather than memorising that runtime.

56
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.520
An exponential output requirement is

57
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.320
concrete evidence for this listing

58
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.300
task, while a slow known

59
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.060
algorithm alone is not a

60
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.940
proof that every algorithm for

61
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.600
another task must be slow.

62
00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.800
A decision problem asks for

63
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.200
yes or no, such as

64
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.420
whether a tour has cost

65
00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.200
at most K. An optimisation

66
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.120
problem instead asks for the

67
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.720
cheapest valid tour or its

68
00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:03.500
minimum cost.

69
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.380
For a spanning tree, the

70
00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.380
decision question asks whether some

71
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.080
spanning tree has total cost

72
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.960
at most K. For zero-one

73
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.060
knapsack, it asks whether some

74
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.540
subset has weight at most

75
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.420
C and profit at least

76
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.820
K, keeping the two inequalities

77
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:19.420
distinct.

78
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.160
An exact optimisation solver can

79
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:24.220
answer its related threshold question

80
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.120
by comparing its optimum with

81
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.820
K. Thus a hardness result

82
00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.300
for the decision version can

83
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.860
imply difficulty for exact optimisation,

84
00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.100
but the formal P and

85
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.980
NP definitions here classify decision

86
00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:37.420
problems.

87
00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.360
Input representation also matters when

88
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:41.800
numbers appear in a problem.

89
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.520
A binary capacity has logarithmic

90
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.160
encoding length in its numeric

91
00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.720
value, so an algorithm looping

92
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.400
over that entire value needs

93
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.540
a different complexity interpretation from

94
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.380
one reading an explicitly listed

95
00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:54.740
string.

96
00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.740
Things to take note of:

97
00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.900
state the input encoding, output

98
00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.740
requirement and objective before classifying

99
00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.880
a task. Distinguish a listing

100
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.060
lower bound from a slow

101
00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.640
known search, preserve threshold inequalities,

102
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.040
and avoid calling every inconvenient

103
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:12.700
problem provably exponential, hmph.
